that we have the best technology

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:35.230
for those things in the world.

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:37.590
The other thing is
being open and having

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:41.890
an open-source-based technology,
or being an open cloud

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:44.190
is super critical,
because all customers

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:47.860
do want a solution that can
run in multiple environments.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:48.480
Right?

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.160
Most of our customers are
in a hybrid environment,

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.060
especially with on premises
in Cloud, or on GCP.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:59.340
Some of them are across
multiple different clouds.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:00.923
So it's important
to have technologies

00:13:00.923 --> 00:13:04.452
that can actually span between
different environments.

00:13:04.452 --> 00:13:06.660
And then, of course, one of
our key differentiators--

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:09.660
we don't talk much about it, but
one of our key differentiators,

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.840
I fundamentally believe,
is our global network.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.340
The reach that we
have with our network

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.920
is pretty amazing in
every part of the world.

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:18.640
And we'll see a bit of that.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:21.690
But before that, let's talk
about the data analytics.

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:23.010
We are a data company.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:25.830
Fundamentally, the whole
Google, as a company,

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.420
we collect massive
amounts of data.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:28.980
We process that data.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:29.950
We analyze it.

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:31.965
And we've built some
amazing products.

00:13:31.965 --> 00:13:33.840
Damian was saying we
have seven products that

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.749
are billion-plus active users.

00:13:35.749 --> 00:13:37.290
We are actually
going to be roughly--

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:40.530
I think eighth one is coming, I
heard earlier in the keynotes,

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:44.965
with the Drive Enterprise
maybe reaching billion users.

00:13:44.965 --> 00:13:45.840
So that's what we do.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.180
We understand users.

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:49.420
We understand what they need.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:51.390
And we build better
products for them.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:54.550
And so big data is in our DNA.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:56.890
One of the other key
learnings that I have--

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:58.680
and this is one of things.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.520
I think "Forbes" or somebody
had the article on this is,

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:04.200
if your company is
not good at analytics,

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:05.940
you are never going
to be good at AI.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:08.070
So fundamentally, the
first thing I always say

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.800
is, get better at
core analytics.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.780
Get better at having good data.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.360
And then you'll be able
to do machine learning

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:16.960
pretty easily on top of that.

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:18.585
And one of the things
that we are doing

00:14:18.585 --> 00:14:20.460
is trying to get the
machine learning tools,

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:23.550
make it easier and easier
for our audience to use them

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.240
and leverage them
for solving problems.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:27.150
And I will touch
upon a couple of them

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.680
this week that we have launched.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:30.540
But from a core
platform perspective,

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.440
what we are trying
to do is four things.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.160
One, we want you to
focus on analytics, not

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:36.490
on infrastructure.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.991
So if you look at some of our
products that we are building,

00:14:38.991 --> 00:14:39.948
they're all serverless.

00:14:39.948 --> 00:14:41.620
So you can literally
bring your data in,

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:44.490
you can start analyzing,
start doing segmentation,

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.307
start doing targeting,
just based on the data

00:14:47.307 --> 00:14:47.890
that you have.

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:49.427
So you don't need skills--

00:14:49.427 --> 00:14:51.510
you don't have to worry
about monitoring products.

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.134
You don't have to
worry about managing,

00:14:53.134 --> 00:14:54.180
scaling infrastructures.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:54.971
That's our problem.

00:14:54.971 --> 00:14:56.457
We will take care of that.

00:14:56.457 --> 00:14:58.290
The second thing is
comprehensive solutions.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:01.170
And I'll touch upon the whole
portfolio of products we have

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:05.030
and how they fit into what you
can do with marketing tech.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.120
I will touch upon
that more details.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.210
We have an end-to-end
machine learning lifecycle.

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:12.540
So if you think about
it, 80% of the time

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.840
our data scientists and analysts
spent on is cleaning data.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:19.746
And 20% goes into actually doing
real analytics or real machine

00:15:19.746 --> 00:15:20.370
learning stuff.

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:21.870
So we're trying to
build tools that

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:25.697
are making it easy for you to go
do that, that whole lifecycle.

00:15:25.697 --> 00:15:27.030
And then, of course, innovation.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:28.080
We're innovating faster.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.032
We're delivering newer
and newer products.

00:15:30.032 --> 00:15:31.740
So this is a whole
portfolio of products.

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:33.720
I won't go into everything here.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:35.580
The key point I wanted
to make in this one

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:40.050
is, if you wanted to collect
time real-time events globally,

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.300
wherever your
customers are, and then

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:45.180
you want to take those events
as soon as a person comes

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:47.110
onto your website, on
your mobile device,

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:51.184
and you want to do a targeted
offer for them or something,

00:15:51.184 --> 00:15:52.600
we have products
across the board.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.570
So if you see
Cloud Pub/Sub, it's

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:57.271
a globally scalable
event collection system

00:15:57.271 --> 00:15:59.520
where any kind of clickstream,
or event, or something,

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:02.400
you can just collect
and then take action on.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:07.410
Dataflow, Dataproc, they're
large scale data processing

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:10.380
solutions, where you
can get those events

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:11.300
in real-time figures.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:12.591
I will give you a real example.

00:16:12.591 --> 00:16:14.400
At Zulily, we did this.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:17.640
Every time first people
came into the website,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.100
we collected those events and
clickstream, collected them

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:22.430
into a messaging system,
real-time aggregated

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:24.180
that information, and
just showed it back.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.280
You see this on different
hotel sites and all.

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:29.130
n number of people are
watching this room right now,

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:31.430
or y a number of people
are buying this stuff now.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:32.150
We just did that.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:34.649
It took us a couple of weeks
because that all pipelines were

00:16:34.649 --> 00:16:35.900
already in place.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:38.120
Immediate uplifting of
our conversion rates,

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:40.550
because that's an instant
feedback for customers

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:41.750
what they are doing.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:43.370
So those kind of
systems are really

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:47.360
easy to build with things like
Pub/Sub, Dataflow, Dataproc.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:49.520
And then BigQuery is a
large-scale data warehouse.

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:50.840
I'll talk more about it.

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:56.030
It allows you to go ahead and
collect large amounts of data,

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:59.630
and actually process it, and
do segmentation and things

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:00.300
like that.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.466
And then if you want to get
into advanced analytics,

00:17:02.466 --> 00:17:05.810
you can use ML Engine,
TensorFlow for real machine

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:07.740
learning at scale.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:09.319
Here's our database
portfolio again.

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:12.491
I'm not going to do a lot of
talk on specific products.

00:17:12.491 --> 00:17:14.240
But the most important
thing, I would say,

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:16.849
is, as I said, BigQuery,
large-scale data

00:17:16.849 --> 00:17:18.510
analytics you can do in that.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.051
The other side of
things is if you're

00:17:20.051 --> 00:17:23.569
looking for a globally scalable
relational database which

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:27.520
can be consistent across the
globe for your transactions,

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:28.760
Spanner is the solution.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:31.110
Spanner can scale globally.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:32.840
A lot of our internal
systems actually

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:35.097
leverage Spanner for the
consistency across the globe.

00:17:35.097 --> 00:17:37.430
So that's one of the products
that you should absolutely

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:38.400
look at.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.400
But let's talk about
BigQuery for a second

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:43.040
because that's where we
have key innovations.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:47.360
So a lot of internal analytics
that we run in Google

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:49.280
all runs on something
called Dremel.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:50.920
There is a Dremel
paper externally.

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:54.180
BigQuery is an externalization
of the same technology.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:58.040
So first of all, you will use
the same petabyte-scale Cloud

00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:59.870
scale data warehouse
that we internally

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:03.420
use for all our analytics, you
will use in your enterprise.

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:04.820
So that's one key thing.

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:08.960
The second key thing here
is, it's highly secure.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:11.270
It allows you to do
real-time streaming directly

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:12.420
into the data warehouse.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:15.050
So you can collect events,
and at the same time,

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:16.940
ingest it directly
into the warehouse,

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:18.680
and do real-time analysis.

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:20.570
Somebody checks into
the hotel, and you

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:22.940
want an analyst to
know that instantly

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:24.010
within under a second.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.510
You can collect that, put it
into BigQuery, know what it is,

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:27.524
and take actions on it.

00:18:27.524 --> 00:18:28.940
So those are the
kind of scenarios

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.430
you can enable with this.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.740
Fully managed and
supports standard SQL.

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:36.860
So most of your analysts
would know some form of SQL.

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:40.155
And if they can use SQL
for general analytics,

00:18:40.155 --> 00:18:43.510
BigQuery is very easily
available for that.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.260
One of the things in
BigQuery we have done

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:49.760
is making it really easy for
you to get your advertising

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:52.760
information data from
different ad channels and all,

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:57.450
especially with Google,
easily into BigQuery.

00:18:57.450 --> 00:19:00.440
For example, most
of our customers

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:04.942
already use Google tech,
AdWords, DoubleClick, YouTube,

00:19:04.942 --> 00:19:06.650
Play Store, all of
these different things

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:07.700
for advertising.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.320
All that data,
with a few clicks,

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:11.140
you can literally
pull it into BigQuery.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:12.290
And so that's one thing.

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:13.915
And then if you have
some of the data--

00:19:13.915 --> 00:19:19.280
we have a lot of companies
that use Drive with Sheets

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.920
and all to create some
campaign information

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:24.450
and stuff like that, which
is like a master information.

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:26.294
So if it is there in
Drive, we can just

00:19:26.294 --> 00:19:28.460
connect to it without even
pulling it into BigQuery.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:30.950
So we do both federation,
which is connecting

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:33.710
to external sources, but also
moving data into BigQuery

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:37.790
easily so that we can analyze
this at scale for you.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:39.980
Other than that, we
literally believe

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:41.300
in democratizing insights.

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:45.020
What we do is, if
one of the analysts

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:48.680
has built segmentation,
a query to go and segment

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:51.470
your customers into
high value, low volume,

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:54.380
medium value based on, let's
say, their lifetime span,

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:57.590
you can go in and share that
with every other analyst

00:19:57.590 --> 00:20:02.090
within the organization using
the query sharing capability.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:04.230
You can build a Data Studio.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:07.190
How many of you have
tried or used Data Studio?

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:08.060
Ah, yeah.

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:10.290
If you haven't, you should
absolutely try it out.

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.650
It's absolutely free.

00:20:12.650 --> 00:20:14.414
The second thing
is, the latest thing

00:20:14.414 --> 00:20:15.830
that we announced
with Data Studio

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:18.480
is prebuilt the
gallery of reports.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:21.110
For example, if you're using
AdWords for advertising,

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:24.380
there's prebuilt reports
in the report gallery

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:26.180
that shows you all
your ads spend,

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:27.777
analyses, and stuff like that.

00:20:27.777 --> 00:20:29.860
But one of the things you
can do is just take that

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:31.276
and connect with
BigQuery to start

00:20:31.276 --> 00:20:33.260
analyzing more
in-depth what it is,

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:36.740
visualize it, and share
it with your customers,

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:39.217
or within your
organization with folks.

00:20:39.217 --> 00:20:41.300
One of the things people
do a lot with Data Studio

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:43.341
is they build those reports
and actually share it

00:20:43.341 --> 00:20:44.882
across organizations.

00:20:44.882 --> 00:20:47.090
So you can take a report,
share it with other people.

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:49.190
They can use their login to
see what is relevant to them

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:50.023
and stuff like that.

00:20:50.023 --> 00:20:52.340
So a really powerful
tool, Sheets integration.

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:54.050
And one of the most
important things

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:56.660
is we have a huge partner
ecosystem that also allows

00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:59.450
you to get data into
BigQuery and analyze it

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:00.660
and stuff like that.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:04.550
So as I said earlier
today, in my introduction,

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:05.360
I was at Zulily.

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:07.940
I was VP of engineering
there for 3 and 1/2 years.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:09.727
I built most of
the data systems.

00:21:09.727 --> 00:21:11.310
Before that, let me
talk about Zulily.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:15.440
Zulily is an online
retailer, fashion retailer.

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:18.200
We launched 9,000
products every day.

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:21.120
They lasted on the
site only for 72 hours.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:23.327
So there is a sense of
urgency to come and buy stuff

00:21:23.327 --> 00:21:24.410
because the deals go away.

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:27.320
It's a daily deal site.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:30.650
More than 5 million
active users when I left.

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.630
That's a public number.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:34.960
And then what is that?

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.280
We used to have millions
of people coming every day,

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:39.900
visiting the site, trying
to look for different offers

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:41.840
and stuff like that.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.090
There are few things which
are really critical, right?

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:46.610
When you have that many people
coming and that many products

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:48.276
launching, it's not
a search-based site.

00:21:48.276 --> 00:21:52.656
It's basically a personalized
discovery kind of an experience

00:21:52.656 --> 00:21:53.780
that you would give people.

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:56.330
You have to show person what
is relevant at the right time,

00:21:56.330 --> 00:21:58.220
at the right moment,
at the right price

00:21:58.220 --> 00:21:59.900
because if you don't
do that, there's

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:03.650
no way of searching through the
catalog, which is so volatile.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:05.790
It's not a fixed catalog system.

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.470
So one of the
things that we did,

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:10.340
which was collect all the
data from our catalog,

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:12.770
from all the different
vendors, and actually go ahead

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:15.980
and personalize the whole
website based on what people

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:18.170
were doing in past,
and leverage machine

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:21.180
learning for doing
a lot of that stuff.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:23.960
The second thing was, we
had 500 merchants or so,

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:25.940
like, really large
merchant population

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:29.420
that was responsible for
bringing the right products in.

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:32.780
We had to be able to give them
right insights in real time--

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:35.540
which customers are buying
what kind of products,

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:37.811
and how do you optimize
that in real time, what

00:22:37.811 --> 00:22:39.810
should be the right prices,
and stuff like that.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:40.920
So that was there.

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:43.710
How do you make better
marketing decisions?

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:47.450
The key thing was, how do
you keep the growth on?

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:53.090
Within six, seven years, we were
a multibillion dollar company.

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:54.680
And that growth rate
was really high.

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:56.150
How do you get
more customers in?

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:57.830
How do you do
better segmentation?

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:00.500
Especially, reducing
your cost of acquisition

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:02.347
was a key priority for us.

00:23:02.347 --> 00:23:04.180
So here is what we did
at a very high level.

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.380
It's a 10,000-foot level thing.

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:08.870
The first thing we
did was we realized

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:10.460
when we started
on the journey, we

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:13.470
need a single source of
truth for our customer.

00:23:13.470 --> 00:23:15.890
So what we did was,
first we went BigQuery.

00:23:15.890 --> 00:23:18.500
We tried a lot of different
technology options,

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:20.510
on premises option.

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:23.797
We tried open source stuff
like Hive, Hadoop clusters,

00:23:23.797 --> 00:23:24.630
and stuff like that.

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:27.290
What we realized was, all of
them were not fast enough.

00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:30.320
We needed interactive-level
analytical capability

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:31.924
for our analysts, right?

00:23:31.924 --> 00:23:34.340
They're not going to sit there
for 25 minutes, 30 minutes,

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:37.430
fire a Hadoop query, then come
back, and then get a coffee,

00:23:37.430 --> 00:23:38.270
come back.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:39.560
It doesn't work.

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:40.840
They were very clear.

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.070
Our marketing analytics team
was like, no, no, no, we

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:44.580
need interactivity that's there.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.130
Otherwise, it's
not going to work.

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.260
So we leverage BigQuery
for all of that.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:52.000
In BigQuery, we created
some key data sets.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.530
There's one data set which
was all about customer.

00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:56.690
So we had all the
customers, our members

00:23:56.690 --> 00:23:58.640
that we had-- members
are customers who

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:00.110
haven't bought anything yet.

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:01.400
Everybody had a row.

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:03.951
We had all the information
for them within a single row.

00:24:03.951 --> 00:24:05.450
And it was like,
how many times have

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:07.908
they bought, what have they
bought, what's the total money.

00:24:07.908 --> 00:24:10.190
All that was in a single
row that we could get.

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:13.059
All their activity was
in a related table,

00:24:13.059 --> 00:24:14.350
so that means you had the user.

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:15.564
You had all the activity.

00:24:15.564 --> 00:24:17.480
All the product and all
this dimensional stuff

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:20.250
was already there.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:23.090
So that was one key thing, like
everything about your customer.

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:26.330
There's a lot of talk about CDPs
now, Customer Data Platforms

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:27.200
and stuff like that.

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:29.090
It's fundamentally
just a central place

00:24:29.090 --> 00:24:31.760
with all your customer
activity information.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:33.830
Once you have that, now
you can do segmentation.

00:24:33.830 --> 00:24:36.620
You can say, hey, tell
me all the customers that

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:39.260
have come in last seven
days multiple times.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:42.620
Tell me all the customers
that came seven multiple times

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:44.690
last week and haven't
come back again.

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:48.290
So we were able to create these
dynamic segments in BigQuery

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:49.380
at scale.

00:24:49.380 --> 00:24:52.370
And that means like the analyst
team and marketing analyst

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:55.700
team could start creating those
and start segmenting them.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:57.530
Because previously,
what we had was,

00:24:57.530 --> 00:25:00.200
hey, we have a lot of customers,
and we send them emails,

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:01.370
and that is it.

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.660
Everybody gets an email
and stuff like that.

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:06.050
But this allowed us to
do micro segmentation

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:07.890
at the scale we could never do.

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:09.440
And so that way,
then those analysts

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:11.750
were able to make those
decisions, segment them,

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:13.980
figuring out anybody who
hadn't come last week,

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:16.430
should we send them an email
and remind them, or maybe

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:17.600
give them an offer.

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:20.954
Or if somebody has been coming
consistently, not buying,

00:25:20.954 --> 00:25:22.370
should we show
different products?

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:26.030
Those kind of decisions were
easier to make at scale.

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:28.730
We had like-- when I
left it, it was more

00:25:28.730 --> 00:25:31.970
than a petabyte scale of data,
the total data set that we had.

00:25:31.970 --> 00:25:34.745
But we started collecting
a lot of information.

00:25:34.745 --> 00:25:36.620
And the second thing,
as I said, we basically

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:38.960
collected real-time
events from the site.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:41.960
As soon as people landed on
the website, all the activity

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:44.090
they did, what products
they were clicking on,

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:45.740
what products they were
buying, what products that

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:46.730
were going in the cart.

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:49.047
We collected all of
that information.

00:25:49.047 --> 00:25:50.630
The statistics, which
is interesting--

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:53.120
it's actually in the case study.

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:56.710
When we started the project
for real-time collection,

00:25:56.710 --> 00:26:00.500
we were collecting 50
million events in a day.

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:02.720
By the time I left,
we were already

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:04.250
at five billion events a day.

00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:06.940
So it was an 100x increase
in number of events.

00:26:06.940 --> 00:26:09.770
The thing is, once you start
seeing value from data,

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:11.550
you want more and
more collection.

00:26:11.550 --> 00:26:14.090
And the data volume just grows.

00:26:14.090 --> 00:26:16.690
So we went from 50 million
to 5 billion a day.

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:20.150
We were peaking at almost 50,000
or 35,000 to 50,000 events

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:21.210
a second.

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.960
But the key thing there was
getting that information

00:26:23.960 --> 00:26:26.990
in real-time ability to
aggregate that information,

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:28.640
push it into the
serving platform,

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:30.950
like website, to go ahead
and see how many people are

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:32.240
watching some products.

00:26:32.240 --> 00:26:34.610
And making decisions like
that became really easy.

00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:36.020
And we didn't have to
worry about anything

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:37.728
because the scaling
of the infrastructure

00:26:37.728 --> 00:26:42.020
was all taken care by the Cloud
infrastructure that we had.

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:44.840
So we basically put all that
data, leveraged Dataproc

00:26:44.840 --> 00:26:46.760
for the cleansing
of data, making sure

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:48.380
that data was
ready for analysts.

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:51.230
And then all the data
was put into BigQuery.

00:26:51.230 --> 00:26:53.150
Analysts had Tableau.

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:56.930
So they used Tableau for their
visualizations or reporting.

00:26:56.930 --> 00:26:58.790
Some amazing reports
you'll see trend lines

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:01.020
about how many people
are coming last year

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:03.110
same day, this day, today.

00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:04.220
What is the change?

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:05.446
Is it going up, down?

00:27:05.446 --> 00:27:07.070
How many people are
ready to churn out?

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:10.130
Because we had a metric
on active customers.

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:13.880
All of that, the analyst
teams could do by themselves.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:17.660
And then, of course, we
also took the same data,

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:20.990
the single view of customer and
their activity and all of that,

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:22.670
and also powered our
operation systems.

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:26.320
We had more than 70 APIs that
were built on top of our data

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:29.210
sets within BigQuery that
allowed different systems

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:32.790
to call in and say, hey, give me
the performance of the supplier

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:36.530
so we can give a better
estimation for our customers

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:38.630
how long it takes to
ship them and all that.

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.820
So all those kind of
things were able to do just

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:44.630
because of how we picked the
infrastructure, leveraging

00:27:44.630 --> 00:27:48.040
BigQuery for a lot of
those kind of scenarios.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:51.020
One of the things we launched
this week was BigQuery ML.

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:52.880
It's a machine learning
capability directly

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:54.410
inside BigQuery.

00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:56.130
What it is is very simple.

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:58.250
You have marketing analysts.

00:27:58.250 --> 00:27:59.750
They know SQL.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:03.610
They had been running SQL
queries, analyzing stuff.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.700
BigQuery ML actually
allows you to do

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:08.270
regression models,
like linear regression,

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:11.570
logistical regression, directly
inside the data warehouse.

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:13.070
So if you have
pulled all your data,

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:15.650
moved it into a data warehouse,
which is already there,

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:17.120
now you can write
two lines of code

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:20.580
to create regression
models and do things.

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:21.770
It's pretty interesting.

00:28:21.770 --> 00:28:24.190
And for prediction, you just
have to say select ML.predict

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:25.398
and you get predictions back.

00:28:25.398 --> 00:28:29.180
So it's as simple as
that from my experience

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:30.650
from an analyst's perspective.

00:28:30.650 --> 00:28:32.830
Here are a few examples.

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:36.170
How may of you saw
Day 2 keynotes?

00:28:36.170 --> 00:28:37.070
OK, Great.

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:39.800
You heard from 20th
Century Fox, how

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:42.350
they were able to go ahead and
do a linear regression to go

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:44.183
ahead and predict which
customers were going

00:28:44.183 --> 00:28:46.726
to come back for "Maze Runner"
compared to all the things

00:28:46.726 --> 00:28:49.100
that they have done in past
and different movies and all.

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:50.920
So that's one example.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:53.420
There were a couple of other
sessions we were talking about.

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:56.150
But simple things like,
you want to go ahead

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:58.580
and do logistical regression
for classification?

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.240
Which of your customers
are high value?

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:02.030
Which ones of them
are going to churn?

00:29:02.030 --> 00:29:05.900
Which ones are going to
be doing the subscription

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:07.760
or not taking the subscription?

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.380
All that kind of
analysis, now you

00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:12.080
can really do it inside and
run the whole machine learning

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:14.600
model within seconds and do it.

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:16.250
One of the other
things is, we also

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:19.160
have our partners like Looker
and all building experiences

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:21.840
to bring all of this
together within it.

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:23.000
So that's the BigQuery ML.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:25.340
We just launched it this week.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:26.930
You should try it
out, see how you

00:29:26.930 --> 00:29:29.718
can leverage that for
different marketing scenarios

00:29:29.718 --> 00:29:32.860
that you have.

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:36.070
Other than that, we have a huge
portfolio for your AI and ML

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:37.150
capabilities.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:39.700
From AutoML capability
for translation

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:43.210
that we launched, our
natural language, as well

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:45.920
as AutoML for vision,
which was already there.

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:49.990
Those are just great tools
for non-technical users

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:53.620
or developers to just move
data in, tag them, and then

00:29:53.620 --> 00:29:56.170
start using the API as a
batch prediction to go in

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:58.570
and figure out what
you want to do.

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:00.994
Additionally, if you have
data scientists and PhDs

00:30:00.994 --> 00:30:02.410
within your
organization, they can

00:30:02.410 --> 00:30:06.460
do complex
neural-network-based modeling.

00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:09.970
You can absolutely use
ML Engine, TensorFlow,

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:13.337
all of those things, to go ahead
and run those kind of models.

00:30:13.337 --> 00:30:14.920
The other key thing
we also have is we

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:17.260
have this thing called
Advanced Solution Lab, where

00:30:17.260 --> 00:30:19.690
we work with key
customers and partners

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:23.890
to work together on solving some
interesting and major problems

00:30:23.890 --> 00:30:26.410
that you may be
facing in partnership.

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:27.840
So that's there.

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:31.420
The next thing I want to touch
upon is open source solutions.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:34.660
Focusing on open source is a
key value proposition for us.

00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:36.610
We want to make
sure all the stuff

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:39.970
that our customers are investing
in, it's possible for them

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:41.740
to leverage them not
just on our cloud,

00:30:41.740 --> 00:30:44.650
but also on the different
environments you have.

00:30:44.650 --> 00:30:47.470
And then containerization,
everybody

00:30:47.470 --> 00:30:50.560
who has been using Kubernetes
or has looked at it,

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:53.110
it's basically one of the
fastest growing open source

00:30:53.110 --> 00:30:53.650
projects.

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:55.030
It started from within Google.

00:30:55.030 --> 00:30:58.689
We are using containers,
making it available.

00:30:58.689 --> 00:31:00.230
I'm not going to go
in depth of this.

00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:05.000
But in 15 years of innovation
in big data, we have been--

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:07.460
before Cloud was there, we
used to actually publish

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.840
all these papers and share
whatever we were learning.

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.860
Now, we are making them
available as different products

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:16.170
like BigQuery, Spanner,
a lot of other products.

00:31:16.170 --> 00:31:19.130
We also have products that
provide managed open source

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:22.070
solutions like Dataproc.

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:24.454
If you have a
Hadoop environment--

00:31:24.454 --> 00:31:26.120
how many people have
Hadoop environments

00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:28.920
within their Hadoop
and Spark stuff?

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:29.490
Good.

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:31.520
So Dataproc has
basically managed

00:31:31.520 --> 00:31:33.400
Hadoop and Spark in the cloud.

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:34.860
So you don't have to worry.

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:36.110
You don't need administrators.

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:37.910
You don't need
management capability.

00:31:37.910 --> 00:31:41.640
Just write your scripts in
Hive or Spark, and just run it.

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:43.910
And we just take care
of the whole noting

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:47.180
of the whole machines, bringing
it down, scaling up and down,

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:47.722
auto scaling.

00:31:47.722 --> 00:31:49.263
Those are all the
investments that we

00:31:49.263 --> 00:31:50.400
are taking from our side.

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:52.750
And then Composer
is Airflow, which

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:57.220
if you need a distributed
orchestration capability,

00:31:57.220 --> 00:32:00.770
pull data from point A, go
ahead and do segmentation,

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:05.446
after segmentation, move it to
AdWords, or Facebook, or GDN,

00:32:05.446 --> 00:32:07.070
and then go do
something else, and send

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:08.660
an email, that kind
of orchestration

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:12.420
you can do in Airflow
pretty easily.

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:15.590
Other than that, some of you
must have seen the Istio demo

00:32:15.590 --> 00:32:16.430
yesterday.

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:17.554
Pretty cool stuff.

00:32:17.554 --> 00:32:19.970
That's what we are doing from
an open source perspective--

00:32:19.970 --> 00:32:23.450
Istio, Kubernetes, TensorFlow
again, for machine learning

00:32:23.450 --> 00:32:26.640
is all open source
from our side.

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:28.160
As I said earlier,
one of the key

00:32:28.160 --> 00:32:31.610
differentiators we have is
our global presence and reach.

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:34.340
Now, basically, if you
look at our global network,

00:32:34.340 --> 00:32:37.610
this is the network that is
powering the billions of users

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:41.090
that we go ahead
provide solutions for.

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:43.010
It's the same
network we are making

00:32:43.010 --> 00:32:45.170
available for our customers.

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:49.580
It's basically, you can locate
services closer to your user.

00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:52.790
Imagine YouTube being watched
in all the different countries,

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.500
how close we need to
be to those users.

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:58.470
That's the same network
that we leverage for GCP.

00:32:58.470 --> 00:32:59.820
It's highly available.

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:02.540
And then, of course, choose
your resources and locations

00:33:02.540 --> 00:33:03.320
wherever you are.

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:05.060
If you want to go
ahead and regionalize

00:33:05.060 --> 00:33:08.720
stuff because of data locality
and all, we provide that.

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:11.750
If you look at our
infrastructure and reach,

00:33:11.750 --> 00:33:14.870
we have hundreds of thousands
of miles of cables, fiber optic

00:33:14.870 --> 00:33:15.920
cables.

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:18.110
We actually have submarines
that actually put down

00:33:18.110 --> 00:33:20.800
all these cables to oceans
and stuff like that.

00:33:20.800 --> 00:33:22.930
So it's pretty cool.

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:25.460
We now are actually
in 15 regions.

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:27.860
There are six more
coming in 2018.

00:33:27.860 --> 00:33:31.070
But we had 15 regions,
55 zones already.

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:34.260
So imagine the whole scale
that we are putting together.

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:37.300
With that, I would like
to call upon my next guest

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:39.158
to talk more.

00:33:39.158 --> 00:33:42.081
IKKIN AHN: Thank you.

00:33:42.081 --> 00:33:42.581
Hi.

00:33:42.581 --> 00:33:46.010
[INAUDIBLE]

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:49.900
So yeah, we build
scalable ad tech SM

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:53.270
to handle global
traffic within two years

00:33:53.270 --> 00:33:55.880
thanks to Google infrastructure.

00:33:55.880 --> 00:34:00.080
So what does our scalability
mean to start-up like us?

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:04.730
We started as a two co-founder
star-up three years ago.

00:34:04.730 --> 00:34:09.170
And for the first year,
actually, we moved seven times.

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:13.040
And now, we started this
year with 20 team members.

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:15.590
Now, we are actually
reaching to 50 team members.

00:34:15.590 --> 00:34:17.960
And we are supporting
enterprise customers

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:21.440
with more than 10,000 employees.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:26.420
So throughout our journey, we
always use our infrastructure

00:34:26.420 --> 00:34:28.020
on top of Google Cloud.

00:34:28.020 --> 00:34:31.420
So no matter we have two team
members, 50 team members,

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:34.969
